ividual animals so the same exact  thing again when those samples are held  at at four degrees Celsius  no loss an MFI signal or diagnostic  value of those samples and for  longer-term storage and this graph shows  out to 90 days but we're out to five  months now again when the samples are  held at minus 20 there's no loss in  signal at all during the times that  we've worked so far so what about those  samples that are being shipped and they  might experience some some high  temperature direct during shipment again  we took samples and on this graph you  see the first column of samples is serum  the second column of samples is the  dried blood spot samples held at room  temperature for 24 hours the third  column is dried blood spot samples held  at 37 degrees Celsius Celsius  essentially at 0% humidity and the last  column then is samples held at 37  degrees Celsius and 95% humidity so  again just like we saw in the previous  graphs the the MFI signal that we're  getting from the dried blood spot sample  for this particular study is the mean of  the group anyways is higher than what we  were getting for serum when those  samples are held at 37 degrees Celsius  in zero percent humidity essentially  we're getting very similar there's very  similar signals to what we were getting  both in the the dried blood spot sample  held at room temperature as well as the  serum all samples were positive however  when those samples were held at at 37  degrees in 95% humidity we lost about 25  percent of the mean MFI signal for the  group so this tells us similar to what  they showed in those human studies that  it's important if you're using BBS to  protect the sample for from high  humidity and in fact simply providing a  silica gel pack  I can pack in with the dried blood spot  samples protects them perfectly from  humidity and there's no loss of signal  when samples are held that way so in  their collaborative studies and again  these samples over a thousand paired  clinical a